<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch10-section-thermal-wind-subsection-temperature-advection" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Temperature advection</title>

    <p>
        As the three alternative forms of the thermal wind given by
        <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />,
        <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential" text="type-global" />,
        and <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential-height" text="type-global" />
        show,
        the thermal wind is the vector difference of the geostrophic wind measured at two pressure levels of interest <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m>,
        where <m>p_1 \gt p_2</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-general" number="yes">
            \vec{v}_T = \vec{v}_g(p_2) - \vec{v}_g(p_1)
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        An example of the geostrophic and thermal wind vectors for
        <m>p_1 = 1000 \, \text{hPa}</m>
        and <m>p_2 = 500 \, \text{hPa}</m>
        for the Northern Hemisphere is shown in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start" text="type-global" /> below.
        Note the geostrophic wind illustrated in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start" text="type-global" />
        <term>veers</term> (turns clockwise)
        with increasing height. 
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start">
        <caption>
            Labeled vectors give an example of the 1000 hPa and 500 hPa geostrophic wind
            (thin purple vectors labeled <m>\vec{v}_{g,1000}</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{g,500}</m>, respectively)
            and the corresponding thermal wind
            (thick black vector labeled <m>\vec{v}_T</m>)
            at a Northern Hemisphere location of interest.
            Since the 1000 hPa pressure level is found at a lower height than the 500 hPa pressure level,
            note the geostrophic wind veers (turns clockwise) with increasing height.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Two geostrophic wind vectors drawn with their tails at the same point
                that turn clockwise with increasing height.
                The resulting thermal wind vector is drawn
                with its tail at the tip of the lower geostrophic wind vector
                and its tip at the tip of the higher geostrophic wind vector.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Since <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential-height" text="type-global" />
        dictate the thermal wind <m>\vec{v}_T</m> flows parallel to thickness contours,
        with lower thickness to its left in the Northern Hemisphere,
        we can annotate 1000<ndash />500 hPa geopotential height thickness contours surrounding <m>\vec{v}_T</m>
        onto <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start" text="type-global" />,
        as shown in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer" text="type-global" /> below.
        Equivalently,
        since <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
        dictates <m>\vec{v}_T</m> flows parallel to isotherms of pressure-weighted column-average temperature <m>\overline{T}</m>,
        with lower temperature to its left in the Northern Hemisphere,
        the contours in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer" text="type-global" /> could be interpreted as isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>.
        <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer" text="type-global" /> reveals that,
        at both the 1000 hPa pressure level and the 500 hPa pressure level,
        the geostrophic wind blows across the thickness contours/isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>
        from the higher-valued contour toward the lower-valued contour.
        This indicates <term>column-average geostrophic warm advection</term>
        since larger thickness is associated with warmer column-average temperature.
        Therefore, we can say
        <em>veering of the geostrophic wind with increasing height in the Northern Hemisphere
        corresponds to column-average geostrophic warm advection</em>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-thermal-wind-veer">
        <caption>
            Reproduction of <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-veer-start" text="type-global" />,
            with two contours of 1000<ndash />500 hPa geopotential height thickness
            separated by increment <m>\delta (\Delta Z)</m>,
            where <m>\delta (\Delta Z) \gt 0</m>,
            annotated parallel to <m>\vec{v}_T</m>,
            with the lower-valued thickness contour to <m>\vec{v}_T</m>'s left as appropriate for the Northern Hemisphere.
            Equivalently,
            these contours can be interpreted as isotherms of pressure-weighted column-average temperature <m>\overline{T}</m>
            separated by increment <m>\delta \overline{T}</m>,
            where <m>\delta \overline{T} \gt 0</m>.
            Both <m>\vec{v}_{g,1000}</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{g,500}</m> flow across the thickness contours/isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>
            from the higher-valued contour toward the lower-valued contour,
            indicating column-average geostrophic warm advection.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch10-thermal-wind-veer.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
                Two geostrophic wind vectors drawn with their tails at the same point
                that turn clockwise with increasing height.
                The resulting thermal wind vector is drawn
                with its tail at the tip of the lower geostrophic wind vector
                and its tip at the tip of the higher geostrophic wind vector.
                Two contours of thickness and column-average temperature are drawn parallel to the thermal wind vector
                with the lower-valued contour to the left of the thermal wind vector as appropriate for the Northern Hemisphere.
                Both geostrophic wind vectors flow across the contours from the higher-valued contour to the lower-valued contour,
                indicating column-average geostrophic warm air advection.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Another example of the geostrophic and thermal wind vectors for <m>p_1 = 1000 \, \text{hPa}</m>
        and <m>p_2 = 5000 \, \text{hPa}</m>
        for the Northern Hemisphere is shown in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back-start" text="type-global" /> below.
        In this case, note the geostrophic wind <term>backs</term> (turns counter-clockwise)
        with increasing height.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-thermal-wind-back-start">
        <caption>
            Labeled vectors give an example of the 1000 hPa and 500 hPa geostrophic wind
            (thin purple vectors labeled <m>\vec{v}_{g,1000}</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{g,500}</m>, respectively)
            and the corresponding thermal wind
            (thick black vector labeled <m>\vec{v}_T</m>)
            at a Northern Hemisphere location of interest.
            Since the 1000 hPa pressure level is found at a lower height than the 500 hPa pressure level,
            note the geostrophic wind backs (turns counter-clockwise) with increasing height.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch10-thermal-wind-back-start.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Two geostrophic wind vectors drawn with their tails at the same point
                that turn counter-clockwise with increasing height.
                The resulting thermal wind vector is drawn
                with its tail at the tip of the lower geostrophic wind vector
                and its tip at the tip of the higher geostrophic wind vector.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Again,
        since <m>\vec{v}_T</m> flows parallel to thickness contours,
        with lower thickness to its left in the Northern Hemisphere,
        we can annotate 1000<ndash />500 hPa geopotential heightthickness contours surrounding <m>\vec{v}_T</m>
        onto <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back-start" text="type-global" />,
        as shown in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back" text="type-global" /> below.
        Equivalently, since <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
        dictates <m>\vec{v}_T</m> flows parallel to isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>,
        with lower temperature to its left in the Northern Hemisphere,
        the contours in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back" text="type-global" />
        could be interpreted as isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>.
        <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back" text="type-global" /> reveals that,
        at both the 1000 hPa pressure level and the 500 hPa pressure level,
        the geostrophic wind blows across the thickness contours/isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>
        from the lower-valued contour toward the higher-valued contour.
        This indicates <term>column-average geostrophic cold advection</term>
        since smaller thickness is associated with colder column-average temperature.
        Therefore, we can say
        <em>backing of the geostrophic wind with increasing height in the Northern Hemisphere
        corresponds to column-average geostrophic cold advection</em>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-thermal-wind-back">
        <caption>
            Reproduction of <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind-back-start" text="type-global" />,
            with two contours of 1000<ndash />500 hPa geopotential height thickness
            separated by increment <m>\delta (\Delta Z)</m>,
            where <m>\delta (\Delta Z) \gt 0</m>,
            annotated parallel to <m>\vec{v}_T</m>,
            with the lower-valued thickness contour to <m>\vec{v}_T</m>'s left as appropriate for the Northern Hemisphere.
            Equivalently,
            these contours can be interpreted as isotherms of pressure-weighted column-average temperature <m>\overline{T}</m>
            separated by increment <m>\delta \overline{T}</m>,
            where <m>\delta \overline{T} \gt 0</m>.
            Both <m>\vec{v}_{g,1000}</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{g,500}</m> flow across the thickness contours/isotherms of <m>\overline{T}</m>
            from the lower-valued contour toward the higher-valued contour,
            indicating column-average geostrophic cold advection.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch10-thermal-wind-back.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
                Two geostrophic wind vectors drawn with their tails at the same point
                that turn counter-clockwise with increasing height.
                The resulting thermal wind vector is drawn
                with its tail at the tip of the lower geostrophic wind vector
                and its tip at the tip of the higher geostrophic wind vector.
                Two contours of thickness and column-average temperature are drawn parallel to the thermal wind vector
                with the lower-valued contour to the left of the thermal wind vector as appropriate for the Northern Hemisphere.
                Both geostrophic wind vectors flow across the contours from the lower-valued contour to the higher-valued contour,
                indicating column-average geostrophic cold air advection.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Note that,
        due to the reversal of the Coriolis force's direction in the Southern Hemisphere relative to its direction in the Northern Hemisphere,
        veering (backing) of the geostrophic wind with increasing height in the Southern Hemisphere
        corresponds to column-average geostrophic cold (warm) advection.
    </p>

</subsection>